{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.linker.md | sed -n '1382,1475p;1912,1947p;1960,1990p;2041,2065p;2128,2160p;2208,2254p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1382\t    snapshots above, and an export a child re-exports carries that\n  1383\t    dependency's `:store-of`, never the child's ambient bindings.\n  1384\t\n  1385\t  **Private engine resources (r8).** r7 let `:store-get` fall back\n  1386\t  to the ambient store for engine-filed resource cells. That\n  1387\t  fallback was a forging vector: `engine/gensym` mints resource\n  1388\t  keys from the VM's counter (`:stream-0`, `:cursor-1`), the ids\n  1389\t  are predictable, and a linked module could emit\n  1390\t  `[:store-get :stream-0]` and read a host handle no publisher\n  1391\t  handed it. The split is therefore physical, not conventional:\n  1392\t  stream handles, cursor cells, and the FFI pair live in a private\n  1393\t  `:resources` table in VM state; the engine's own machinery\n  1394\t  (wait-set resolution, handle creation and polling, resume) reads\n  1395\t  and writes only that table; and no user store instruction and no\n  1396\t  `resolve-var` step consults it, at top level or inside a module\n  1397\t  closure. The ids may stay predictable -- they name nothing a\n  1398\t  store instruction can reach. This amends the store model UCF\n  1399\t  section 7.6.2 itself states -- that section opens by listing what\n  1400\t  the store S holds (listed with the other amendments in section\n  1401\t  11, item 12; r8's citation of a \"UCF 4.1\" named no such section,\n  1402\t  corrected r10): a migration slice still carries resource\n  1403\t  cells, remapped at resume, but beside the program store, never\n  1404\t  inside it.\n  1405\t\n  1406\t  **Resource lowering (r9).** The continuation format's own decode\n  1407\t  rules move with the split, or the private table is bypassed at\n  1408\t  every boundary crossing: UCF section 7.5.1's table decodes a\n  1409\t  `:yin.k/stream` marker to \"an attached handle, under a fresh\n  1410\t  store key\" and a `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to \"a store cursor entry\",\n  1411\t  and section 7.5.3 lowers each logical cell to \"a fresh store\n  1412\t  cursor entry\". From r9 those decode targets are the private\n  1413\t  table, as an amendment to UCF sections 7.5.1 and 7.5.3 (added to\n  1414\t  the list in section 11, item 12): lowering installs an attached\n  1415\t  handle under a fresh **resource id** in `:resources`, lowers a\n  1416\t  logical cell to a cursor entry in `:resources` seeded with its\n  1417\t  carried position, and remaps every `:yin.k/cursor-ref` to that\n  1418\t  entry; two refs to one cell still share one entry. Program\n  1419\t  values keep only the non-forgeable references -- the opaque\n  1420\t  `stream-ref` and `cursor-ref` ids, exactly as a running program\n  1421\t  holds them -- and no store key is ever created. A module export\n  1422\t  carrying a stream or cursor reference therefore lifts and lowers\n  1423\t  through the same rules: the marker crosses in the slice, the\n  1424\t  receiver's `:resources` gains the attachment or cell, and the\n  1425\t  export's value in the receiver is the remapped reference,\n  1426\t  indistinguishable from one the receiver made itself.\n  1427\t\n  1428\t  **Sealed references (r10).** r9 moved resources out of the store,\n  1429\t  but a reference is still a plain map with a predictable id\n  1430\t  (`{:type :stream-ref, :id :stream-0}`), and an effect carries\n  1431\t  whatever value the program supplies: a program or linked module\n  1432\t  can write the literal itself and name a resource it was never\n  1433\t  handed -- another task's stream, a cursor cell, or the FFI pair's\n  1434\t  ids. Shape checks are not the answer (UCF 7.5.1 refuses every\n  1435\t  \"looks like data\" rule); unguessability is. Each task's resources\n  1436\t  are bound to a **task-scoped capability secret**, minted once by\n  1437\t  the composition at task creation from its own random source and\n  1438\t  held in VM state -- never in a program value, never on a stream,\n  1439\t  never counter-derived. Every reference the engine issues -- a\n  1440\t  `stream-ref`, a `cursor-ref`, an FFI cell id -- carries a seal\n  1441\t  over its id under that secret, and every effect dispatch that\n  1442\t  resolves a program-supplied reference verifies the seal against\n  1443\t  the active task's secret before touching `:resources`. A literal\n  1444\t  with a wrong or missing seal fails closed with\n  1445\t  `:forged-resource-reference`, naming the effect and the id it\n  1446\t  named: the effect boundary, not the store instruction, was the\n  1447\t  last read path, and r10 closes it. Lift and lower re-seal rather\n  1448\t  than carry: the lift encodes a `:yin.k/stream` or\n  1449\t  `:yin.k/cursor-ref` marker without the emitter's seal (the\n  1450\t  marker is the portable encoding), and the lower, having\n  1451\t  installed the attachment or cell in the receiver's `:resources`,\n  1452\t  issues a fresh reference sealed under the receiver's secret --\n  1453\t  which is what r9's \"remapped reference\" now means. One task's\n  1454\t  literals cannot guess another task's secret, so cross-task\n  1455\t  forgery fails with the same refusal. This amends UCF section\n  1456\t  7.5.1's reference markers (added to the list in section 11,\n  1457\t  item 12).\n  1458\t  Executing exports inside the owning child instead was considered and\n  1459\t  rejected: it keeps a child VM alive for the parent's lifetime, and\n  1460\t  every application of an export becomes a function call across a VM\n  1461\t  boundary, which is the coupling the axioms forbid. Relocation costs\n  1462\t  one lift and one lower per install and nothing afterwards, and it is\n  1463\t  the same machinery UCF already needs, exercised in-process. The\n  1464\t  offset table it needs is one vector of `[identity offset length]` per\n  1465\t  positional kernel, appended to by `attach-image`; `yin.repl` already\n  1466\t  computes the offset and discards it, so this is recording a fact the\n  1467\t  loader has in hand, not a new computation. `attach-image` itself is\n  1468\t  the one new kernel operation this section asks for, and it is the\n  1469\t  append half of an operation `yin.repl` already performs.\n  1470\t- **`refused` restores every waiter with the refusal** as the effect's\n  1471\t  error, and the install entry is removed so a later require may try\n  1472\t  again.\n  1473\t\n  1474\tThe alternative, per-format export locators in the manifest (a body pc\n  1475\tfor stack images, a segment and pc for semantic ones), was considered\n  1912\tparent, a parent binding of the same name neither supplies nor\n  1913\tshadows it, and a write from one export is visible to the next\n  1914\tapplication in the same task and to no other task; a read inside a\n  1915\tbody applied before its definition retains its obligation and is\n  1916\t`:undeclared-free` unless the manifest declares it; an export-only\n  1917\timage with no application site produces no obligations; a parked\n  1918\tentry recorded before an attach restores after it at the same pcs\n  1919\twith `:segment` never assigned from the entry; a register call in\n  1920\tflight while a nested require attaches an image returns into the\n  1921\tgrown code space, the attachment intact after the return; two tasks\n  1922\tholding images of different lengths require the same module and each\n  1923\tapplies its export correctly; a `:store-put` executed inside an\n  1924\texported closure writes the module store, and a module closure\n  1925\tcalling another module's closure writes each module's own store; a\n  1926\tslice re-exporting a dependency closure carries the dependency's\n  1927\tstore snapshot with the child's mutations, and a task already\n  1928\tholding that dependency's store keeps its own instance; a module\n  1929\tforging a predictable resource key (`[:store-get :stream-0]`)\n  1930\tagainst a task with a live stream reads nothing on any backend; a\n  1931\tforged reference literal -- stream, cursor, or FFI cell id --\n  1932\tpassed to an effect is `:forged-resource-reference` while the\n  1933\ttask's own sealed references resolve, and a lifted-then-lowered\n  1934\treference works under the receiver's seal; a module export\n  1935\tcarrying a stream or cursor reference lifts and\n  1936\tlowers, the attachment or cell landing in the receiver's private\n  1937\t`:resources` and only the remapped reference in program values;\n  1938\tREPL input following a module closure's tail-completed application\n  1939\tdefines into the task's own store, no stale module context; a\n  1940\twalker closure whose body node is shared by two `:lambda` rows\n  1941\twith different params lifts from its recorded source row and\n  1942\trefuses `:unrooted-body` when a fabricated `[node params]` pair\n  1943\tmatches no row; `(vm :stack)` and `(vm :register)` in `yin.repl` link\n  1944\tthe same manifest by H and R respectively and produce B0-equal results.\n  1945\t\n  1946\t**M5. `yin.repl` wiring.** The REPL composes the link pair per VM and\n  1947\tthe content pair per connection (`connect` already opens the RPC\n  1960\t          test/yin/vm/debruijn_linker_test.cljc\n  1961\t            -> test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc           (M1)\n  1962\tNew:      test/yin/vm/linker_step_test.cljc           (M3)\n  1963\t          test/yin/vm/linker_authority_test.cljc      (M4 entry)\n  1964\t          test/yin/vm/linker_require_test.cljc        (M4)\n  1965\tEdited:   src/cljc/yin/vm/module.cljc                 (M4)\n  1966\t          src/cljc/yin/vm/engine.cljc  (:link-request, :link-response,\n  1967\t                                        :install wait reasons; the\n  1968\t                                        install child; module-store\n  1969\t 