{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/design/yin.vm.universal-continuation-format.md | sed -n '546,571p;708,715p'\"","aggregated_output":"   546\tslice of §7.6.2. The outcome is either the portable form or a refusal naming\n   547\tthe path.\n   548\t\n   549\tThe first draft encoded markers as ordinary maps — `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`,\n   550\t`{:yin.k/ref addr}` — while passing program map literals through in their\n   551\tnatural shape. That is ambiguous to everyone: §2.5 admits map literals,\n   552\tso a program can *contain* `{:yin.k/primitive '+}`, and a decoder cannot tell\n   553\tan encoder's marker from a program's data. The grammar here is disjoint by\n   554\tconstruction: **every map at an encoded-value position is a UCF marker**, and\n   555\ta program's own maps are always wrapped.\n   556\t\n   557\t| Encoded form | Tag | Contents | Decodes to |\n   558\t|---|---|---|---|\n   559\t| scalars: nil, boolean, number, string, keyword, symbol | — | themselves (never a map; cannot forge a marker) | themselves |\n   560\t| vector, list, set | — | same shape, elements recursively encoded (never a map) | same shape, elements decoded |\n   561\t| literal map | `:yin.k/literal` | `:yin.k/entries [k₁ v₁ k₂ v₂ …]`, keys and values recursively encoded | the map, keys and values decoded |\n   562\t| closure | `:yin.k/closure` | `:yin.k/segment addr :yin.k/entry pc :yin.k/params […] :yin.k/env {encoded}` | `{:type :closure …}` (§2.4) |\n   563\t| reified continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame (§7.4.1), recursively | `{:type :reified-continuation …}` |\n   564\t| parked continuation | `:yin.k/frame` | a nested frame plus `:yin.k/parked-id` | `{:type :parked-continuation …}` |\n   565\t| primitive | `:yin.k/primitive` | `:yin.k/name sym` | the named function, after profile check (§7.5.2) |\n   566\t| stream reference | `:yin.k/stream` | `:dao.stream/identity … :dao.stream/descriptor …` | an attached handle, under a fresh store key |\n   567\t| cursor reference | `:yin.k/cursor-ref` | `:yin.k/cell cell-id` | a store cursor entry for that cell, §7.5.3 |\n   568\t| shared subvalue | `:yin.k/ref` | `:yin.k/id addr` | the table entry at `addr`, §7.5.3 |\n   569\t| anything else | — | — | **refused**: `:yin.k/non-portable` |\n   570\t\n   571\tWhy the wrapping is total rather than an \"escaping rule\" for suspicious\n   708\tA stream reference is portable iff its transport produces a descriptor whose\n   709\t`attach!` can succeed somewhere other than the emitter's host. The encoder\n   710\tcannot know that; it encodes the descriptor and lets the resumer's `attach!`\n   711\treport `not-found` as data. What the encoder *can* refuse is a handle whose\n   712\t`descriptor` call fails, which cannot happen under the contract, and a raw\n   713\tin-memory handle admitted by an in-process stream's admission declaration\n   714\t(`dao.stream.md`, *Creation and Attachment*), which it treats as a host\n   715\tobject.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
